 ## Overview
-This method prevents the loss of geometric detail in adaptive mesh volume rendering by replacing small, easily missed polyhedral cells with anti-aliased ellipsoidal splats [S1][S3, unofficial]. While standard slicing renders large cells as polygons, this technique detects cells that are too small to be reliably sampled by pixel centers or slice planes and renders them as Gaussian ellipsoids [S2]. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Threshold Slicing and Anti-Aliasing](1447-threshold-slicing-and-anti-aliasing.md) by explicitly using ellipsoidal splats convolved with a Gaussian presampling filter to handle the anti-aliasing, rather than relying solely on polygon rasterization or post-processing blurs [S1][S3, unofficial].
+This method prevents the loss of geometric detail in adaptive meshes by rendering small volumetric cells as anti-aliased ellipsoidal splats rather than polygons, addressing the problem where small cells are missed between slice planes or by pixel samples during scan conversion [S1, S2][S3, unofficial]. It distinguishes itself from sibling methods by replacing complex filtered polygon rendering with Gaussian ellipsoid splats convolved with a pre-sampling filter, allowing efficient hardware-accelerated compositing via texture mapping and stencil testing [S1][S3, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-The method integrates with a slicing-based volume renderer. It operates on unstructured or curvilinear grids where cells vary significantly in size [S1].
+**Inputs and Outputs:**
+*   **Input:** A set of volumetric cells (polyhedra) from an adaptive or curvilinear grid, a volume threshold $V_{thresh}$, a transfer function mapping cell properties to color/opacity, and a slicing direction.
+*   **Output:** A 2D image where small cells are rendered as anti-aliased splats and large cells as polygons, correctly composited.
 
