-### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Precision:** The subpixel location of the center of gravity is used to analytically compute splat weights. Loss of precision here may affect the accuracy of the compositing opacity [S1].
-*   **Boundaries:** The stencil test must correctly handle the boundaries between large cell polygons and splat regions. If the stencil test is not restricted to regions where polygons are absent, splats may incorrectly overlap opaque regions, causing visual artifacts [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Degenerate Input:** If a cell has zero volume, it should be ignored or handled as a degenerate case. Not given in the sources.
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Precision:** The method relies on accurate volume calculation for thresholding. If the Jacobian varies rapidly, the assumption that splats sum to near unity may break down, leading to interpolation errors [S1, S2][S3, unofficial].
+*   **Boundaries:** The stencil test must correctly handle the boundaries between large and small cells to avoid gaps or overlaps. If the stencil test is not properly restricted, splats may overwrite large polygons or vice versa [S1, S2][S3, unofficial].
+*   **Degenerate Input:** Cells with zero volume or degenerate geometry may cause issues in ellipsoid fitting. Not given in the sources.
 
