 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Preprocessing involves sorting and projecting all small cells, which is $O(N \log N)$ for sorting and $O(N)$ for projection, where $N$ is the number of small cells. Interactive rendering is efficient as splats are pre-computed into texture maps.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing texture maps for all slices. The size depends on the number of slices and the size of the bounding rectangles. Not given in the sources.
-*   **Strengths:** Prevents loss of detail in adaptive meshes; handles small cells that would otherwise be missed; efficient interactive rendering via texture mapping.
-*   **Limitations:** Requires preprocessing; complex to implement due to the need for ellipsoid fitting and stencil buffer management; relies on hardware texture mapping capabilities.
-*   **When to use:** When rendering adaptive meshes with significant variation in cell size, and small cells contain important geometric details.
-*   **When not to use:** When cells are uniformly large, or when pre-processing time is prohibitive.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Preprocessing involves sorting and rendering small cells to an image, which is $O(N \log N)$ for sorting and $O(N)$ for rendering, where $N$ is the number of small cells. Interactive rendering is $O(L + S)$, where $L$ is the number of large cells and $S$ is the number of small cell rectangles.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for texture maps, which can be significant if many small cells are packed into textures. Not given in the sources.
+*   **Measured Performance:** The method allows for real-time interactivity by leveraging hardware texture mapping and stencil testing. It avoids the complexity of rendering filtered polygons. Not given in the sources.
+*   **Strengths:** Prevents loss of detail in adaptive meshes; efficient hardware acceleration; smooth interpolation in curvilinear grids.
+*   **Limitations:** Requires preprocessing; texture map management can be complex; relies on accurate volume calculation.
+*   **When to use:** When rendering adaptive meshes with small cells that might be missed by standard slicing.
+*   **When not to use:** When cells are uniformly large or when pre-sampling filters are sufficient.
 
 ## Background
-This method was developed for volume rendering on curvilinear and unstructured grids, specifically to address the issue of small cells in adaptive meshes being missed during slice-based rendering [S1][S3, unofficial]. It builds on the work of Williams et al. [8] regarding spine splats and Zwicker et al. [5] regarding Gaussian presampling filters [S1][S3, unofficial]. The technique was presented in the context of interactive visualization of large datasets, requiring parallelization for performance [S1][S3, unofficial].
+The method was developed to address the limitations of standard slicing techniques for adaptive meshes, where small cells are often missed. It builds on the work of Williams et al. [8] and Zwicker et al. [5], integrating splatting into the slicing pipeline to handle small cells. The approach is particularly relevant for volume rendering of curvilinear and unstructured grids [S1, S2][S3, unofficial].
 
